#: {{synonyms|en|back-of-the-fag-packet<q:UK>|back-of-the-napkin<q:US>}}
#: {{ux|en|We can do some '''back-of-the-envelope''' calculations before all the facts come in.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Edgar Cortright|Edgar M[aurice] Cortright]], witness|chapter=Statement of Edgar M. Cortright, Deputy Associate Administrator for Space Science and Applications, NASA; Accompanied by Dr. John F. Clark, Director of Sciences, Office of Space Sciences and Applications|title=1965 NASA Authorization: Hearings before the Subcommittee on Space Sciences and Applications of the [[w:United States House Committee on Science, Space, and Technology|Committee on Science and Astronautics]], [[w:United States House of Representatives|U.S. House of Representatives]], Eighty-eighth [[w:United States Congress|Congress]], Second Session on H.R. 9641 (Superseded by H.R. 10456) {{...|February 17, 18, 19, 20, 21, 25, 26, 27, 28; March 2, 3, 1964}} No. 1, Part 3{{nb...|Printed for the Use of the Committee on Science and Astronautics}}|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|U.S. Government Printing Office]]|date=20 February 1964|page=1548|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Nek1AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA1548|oclc=231024754|passage=[T]he original estimates are frequently '''back-of-the-envelope'''-type estimates made internally without detailed engineering studies, if you go all the way back to Day 1.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Arthur Melvin Okun|Arthur M[elvin] Okun]], witness|chapter=Council of Economic Advisers: Salaries and Expenses|title=Departments of Treasury and Post Office and Executive Office Appropriations for 1967: Hearings before a Subcommittee of the [[w:United States House Committee on Appropriations|Committee on Appropriations]], [[w:United States House of Representatives|House of Representatives]], Eighty-ninth [[w:United States Congress|Congress]], Second Session{{nb...|[...] Printed for the Use of the Committee on Appropriations}}|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|U.S. Government Printing Office]]|date=17 March 1966|page=713|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=JaQuAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA713|oclc=1328840326|passage=We joke about having to make '''back-of-the-envelope''' calculations, while in the university environment we might spend 2 or 3 weeks computerizing the calculations. I might say, however, that making '''back-of-the-envelope''' calculations and later checking them out on computers has impressed me that we have developed a considerable skill in '''back-of-the-envelope''' calculations.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Thomas Eagleton|Thomas F[rancis] Eagleton]]|chapter=Department of Labor: Labor-management Services Administration: Statement of Jack A. Warshaw, Deputy Assistant Secretary for Labor-management Relations [...]|title=Departments of Labor and Health, Education and Welfare and Related Agencies Appropriations for Fiscal Year 1980: Hearings before a Subcommittee of the [[w:United States Senate Committee on Appropriations|Committee on Appropriations]], {{w|United States Senate}}, Ninety-sixth [[w:United States Congress|Congress]], First Session: Part 1{{nb...|[...] Printed for the Use of the Committee on Appropriations}}|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|U.S. Government Printing Office]]|date=22 February 1979|page=464|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=gLHRAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA464|oclc=938793944|passage=On one defense item, somebody kept referring to it as a '''back-of-an-envelope''' cost estimate. Do you have a '''back-of-an-envelope''' cost estimate?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Tony Aspromourgos|title=On the Origins of Classical Economics: Distribution and Value from William Petty to {{w|Adam Smith}}|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=PphtRyMNLTIC&pg=PT197|series=Routledge Studies in the History of Economics|location=Abingdon, Oxfordshire&#59; New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Routledge|year=1996|year_published=2000|isbn=978-0-415-12878-0|issn=1359-7906|passage=Apart from those manuscripts which were intended as more or less complete, self-contained works, [[w:William Petty|[William] Petty]] left a huge body of papers, much of which amounts to fragments and hastily written private notes&nbsp;– many the seventeenth-century equipvament of '''back-of-the-envelope''' calculations and comments. These papers constitute a further testament to his sustained investigation of economic and political subjects.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Thomas William Körner|T[homas] W[illiam] Körner]]|chapter=Biology in a Darkened Room|title=The Pleasures of Counting|location=Cambridge, Cambridgeshire|publisher=w:Cambridge University Press|year=1996|year_published=2002|section=part II (Meditations on Measurement)|pages=112–113|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=wUdtVHBr-OQC&pg=PA112|isbn=978-0-521-56087-0|passage=The reader will not need to be warned that the calculations involved are of the '''back of an envelope''' type and statements of the form ‘''a'' equals ''b''’ should be read ‘''a'' equals ''b'' approximately’ or even ‘with a bit of luck, ''a'' and ''b'' will be of the same order of magnitude’.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Paul Lawrence Rose|chapter=The Bomb as Reactor: The U235 Bomb Misconceived, 1940|title=Heisenberg and the Nazi Atomic Bomb Project: A Study in German Culture|location=Berkeley&#59; Los Angeles, Calif.&#59; London|publisher=w:University of California Press|year=1998|page=115|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8bgwDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA115|isbn=978-0-520-21077-6|passage=Misapplying a basic feature of diffusion theory, [[w:Werner Heisenberg|[Werner] Heisenberg]] arrived at an impossibly high figure for a critical mass of pure U235. This was done by means of a seductively simple "'''back of an envelope'''" calculation in 1940, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:David Wolf (astronaut)|David A[lexander] Wolf]]|title=Wow! The Earth! Just a Little E-mail from a Guy in Outer Space|editor=w:Peter Warshall|journal=[[w:Whole Earth Review|Whole Earth: Access to Tools, Ideas, and Practices]]|location=San Rafael, Calif.|publisher=[[w:Point Foundation (environment)|Point Foundation]]|month=winter|year=1999|page=89|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_whole-earth_whole-earth_winter-1999_99/page/n94/mode/1up|column=1|issn=0749-5056|oclc=1166829936|passage='''Back of the envelope''' calculation: I've traveled roughly 17 million miles since we left the crew quarters at Cape Kennedy, not including the van ride to the pad&nbsp;… in fact, Earth seems a bit dreamlike these days, as we are connected only by crackling voices on the radio and the photographs brought along and our memories.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=David G. Thomson|quotee=w:Pradeep Sindhu|title=Mastering the 7 Essentials of High-Growth Companies: Effective Lessons to Grow Your Business|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Mq6qABNv_IIC&pg=PT73|location=Hoboken, N.J.|publisher=[[w:Wiley (publisher)|John Wiley & Sons]]|year=2010|isbn=978-0-470-61062-6|passage=I remember very distinctly in October 1995, after I completed my paper design, I had an epiphany. This '''back-of-the-envelope''' design was five years ahead of its time.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:Marshall Kirk McKusick|Kirk McKusick]]; Sean Quinlan|quotee=Sean Quinlan|title=GFS [Google File System]: Evolution on Fast-forward|editor=[[w:Moshe Vardi|Moshe Y[a’akov] Vardi]]|journal=w:Communications of the ACM|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Association for Computing Machinery|ACM Media]]|month=March|year=2010|volume=53|issue=5|page=45|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/communicationsof5303asso/page/45/mode/1up|column=3|doi=10.1145/1666420.1666439|issn=0001-0782|oclc=909095143|passage=[I]t quickly became apparent that the natural mapping we had thought of—and which seemed to make perfect sense back when we were doing our '''back-of-the-envelope''' estimates—turned out not to be acceptable at all.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Christian Wolmar|title=Railways Must Sell Themselves and Offer a Better Product|journal=w:The Railway Magazine|location=Sutton, London|publisher=[[w:TI Media|IPC Transport Press]]|date=24 March 2021|issue=927|page=46|issn=0033-8923|oclc=1065264231|passage=Now, those Treasury mandarins unfamiliar with the railway might assume on the '''back of an envelope''' that if only 80% of trains are running, then that will cost 20% less. If only it were that simple.|brackets=on}}

